"China’s global role: US views in 2025 | Pew Research Center (https://www.pewresearch.org/global/2025/04/17/views-of-chinas-role-in-the-world/)\nciteturn53view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.pewresearch.org/global/2025/04/17/views-of-chinas-role-in-the-world/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 292\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: Numbers, Facts and Trends Shaping Your World\nL3: \nL4:   * 【1†Newsletters】\nL5:   * 【2†Press】\nL6:   * 【3†My Account】\nL7:   * 【4†Donate†pewresearchcenter.networkforgood.com】\nL8:   * 【5†Contacted By Us?】\nL9: \nL10: Read our research on:\nL11: \nL12:   * 【6†Artificial Intelligence】\nL13:   * 【7†Donald Trump】\nL14:   * 【8†Middle East】\nL15: \nL16: [Input: Search pewresearch.org...]\nL17: \nL18:   * Research Topics\nL19: ##### Topics\nL20: \nL21: 【9†Politics & Policy】\nL22: \nL23: 【10†International Affairs】\nL24: \nL25: 【11†Immigration & Migration】\nL26: \nL27: 【12†Race & Ethnicity】\nL28: \nL29: 【13†Religion】\nL30: \nL31: 【14†Age & Generations】\nL32: \nL33: 【15†Gender & LGBTQ】\nL34: \nL35: 【16†Family & Relationships】\nL36: \nL37: 【17†Economy & Work】\nL38: \nL39: 【18†Science】\nL40: \nL41: 【19†Internet & Technology】\nL42: \nL43: 【20†News Habits & Media】\nL44: \nL45: 【21†Methodological Research】\nL46: \nL47: 【22†Full Topic List】\nL48: ##### Regions & Countries\nL49: \nL50: 【23†Asia & the Pacific】\nL51: \nL52: 【24†Europe & Russia】\nL53: \nL54: 【25†Latin America】\nL55: \nL56: 【8†Middle East & North Africa】\nL57: \nL58: 【26†North America】\nL59: \nL60: 【27†Sub-Saharan Africa】\nL61: \nL62: 【28†Multiple Regions / Worldwide】\nL63: ##### Formats\nL64: \nL65: 【29†Feature】\nL66: \nL67: 【30†Fact Sheet】\nL68: \nL69: 【31†Video】\nL70: \nL71: 【32†Data Essay】\nL72: \nL73:   * 【33†Publications】\nL74:   * 【34†Our Methods】\nL75:   * 【35†Short Reads】\nL76:   * 【36†Tools & Datasets】\nL77:   * 【37†Experts】\nL78:   * 【38†About Us】\nL79: \nL80:   *     * 【39†Research Topics】\nL81:     * 【33†Publications】\nL82:     * 【35†Short Reads】\nL83:     * 【40†Tools & Datasets】\nL84:     * 【38†About Pew Research Center】\nL85:     * 【41†Newsletters】\nL86:     * 【42†Press】\nL87:     * 【43†My Account】\nL88:     * 【5†Contacted By Us?】\nL89: \nL90:   * [Input: ]\nL91: \nL92: Read Our Research On:\nL93:     * 【6†Artificial Intelligence】\nL94:     * 【7†Donald Trump】\nL95:     * 【8†Middle East】\nL96: \nL97:   * Report\nL98: \nL99: |\nL100: \nL101: April 17, 2025\nL102: #### 【44†Negative Views of China Have Softened Slightly Among Americans】\nL103: # 2. Views of China’s role in the world\nL104: \nL105: 【45†Add Us On Google†www.google.com】\nL106: \nL107: By 【46†Christine Huang】, 【47†Laura Silver】 and 【48†Laura Clancy】\nL108: \nL109: Table of Contents\nL110: \nL111:   1. 【44†Negative Views of China Have Softened Slightly Among Americans】\nL112:   2. 【49†1. U.S. views of China and Xi】\nL113:   3. 【50†2. Views of China’s role in the world】\nL114:   4. 【51†3. Views of trade between China and the U.S.】\nL115:   5. 【52†4. Views of China as a competitor and threat to the U.S.】\nL116:   6. 【53†Acknowledgments】\nL117:   7. 【54†Methodology】\nL118: Most Americans say China’s international influence has been growing stronger in recent years. Still, Americans tend to say the U.S. – not China – is the world’s top economic and military power.\nL119: ### Americans see China’s global influence getting stronger\nL120: \nL121: A 73% majority of Americans say China’s influence in the world has been getting stronger in recent years. A similar share (71%) held this view in 2024.\nL122: \nL123: About two-in-ten (19%) say China’s influence has stayed about the same, while 6% say it’s getting weaker.\nL124: #### Views by party\nL125: \nL126: Unlike many other opinions related to China, there are no significant differences between Republicans and Democrats on this question. About equal shares in both parties say that China is growing stronger (71% vs. 75%).\nL127: \nL128: #### Views by age\nL129: \nL130: Americans ages 50 and older are slightly more likely than adults under 50 to say China’s global influence is getting stronger (76% vs. 70%).\nL131: ### Which country is the leading economic power?\nL132: \nL133: About half of Americans (48%) think the U.S. is the world’s leading economic power. Another 38% say it’s China, and smaller shares name Japan (7%) or the EU (6%).\nL134: \nL135: These views are largely unchanged since we last asked this question in 2023, though the share naming the U.S. as the world’s top economic power has been as high as 54% in 2020.\nL136: #### Views by party\nL137: \nL138: The stability of these views overall hides major partisan shifts that have occurred over the past two years.\nL139: In 2023, Republicans were about equally likely to describe the U.S. (46%) and China (45%) as the world’s leading economy. Among Republicans, the share naming the U.S. as the top economy has gone up 12 points, while the share naming China has fallen by about the same amount. Today, a clear majority of Republicans (58%) name the U.S. as the world’s top economic power, compared with 32% who name China.\nL140: Democrats are now about equally likely to name China (43%) and the U.S. (40%) as the world’s leading economic power. By comparison, 52% of Democrats said the U.S. was the top economy in 2023, and 33% said China.\nL141: #### Views by age\nL142: \nL143: Older Americans are less likely than younger people to say China is the world’s leading economic power. Among those ages 65 and older, 27% name China as the top economy – a much larger share (60%) name the U.S. In comparison, adults under 30 are about equally likely to see China and the U.S. as the world’s top economic power.\nL144: ### Which country is the leading military power?\nL145: \nL146: Most Americans (76%) say the U.S. is the world’s leading military power. Fewer say the top military power is China (14%) or Russia (8%).\nL147: \nL148: Compared with 2022, Americans are more likely to see the U.S. as the world’s leading military power (+6 points), but they are still less likely than they were in 2020 to hold this view.\nL149: #### Views by party\nL150: \nL151: Republicans are more likely than Democrats to see the U.S. as the world’s leading military power, though majorities of both parties hold this view (80% and 73%).\nL152: \nL153: This is a reversal from 2022, when Democrats were more likely than Republicans to see the U.S. as the leading military power (75% vs. 67%) and less likely than Republicans to name China (16% vs. 22%).\nL154: \nL155: 【51†Next: Views of trade between China and the U.S.】\nL156: \nL157: 【49†← Prev Page】\nL158: \nL159: 【44†1】【49†2】3【51†4】【52†5】【53†6】【54†7】\nL160: 【51†Next Page →】\nL161: \nL162:   * 【55†Report PDF】\nL163:   * 【56†Topline】\nL164:   * 【57†Questionnaire】\nL165:   * 【58†American Trends Panel Wave 166】\nL166: \nL167: 【59†Image: Icon for promotion number 1】\nL168: ### Sign up for our weekly newsletter\nL169: \nL170: Fresh data delivered Saturday mornings\nL171: \nL172: [Input]\nL173: \nL174: Go back to previous question Reset\nL175: \nL176: Thank you for subscribing!\nL177: \nL178: Processing…\nL179: \nL180: Topics\nL181: \nL182:   * 【60†China Global Image】\nL183:   * 【61†Global Balance of Power】\nL184:   * 【62†Global Economy & Trade】\nL185:   * 【63†Global Trade】\nL186:   * 【10†International Affairs】\nL187: \nL188: ### Related\nL189: ### Most Popular\nL190: \nL191: 1\nL192: \nL193: 【64†Americans’ Use of Social Media in 2026】\nL194: \nL195: 2\nL196: \nL197: 【65†Women, Work and Caregiving Through the Arc of Family Life】\nL198: \nL199: 3\nL200: \nL201: 【66†Religious Groups Differ Widely in Their 2026 Midterm Preferences】\nL202: \nL203: 4\nL204: \nL205: 【67†Quiz: What Is Your Civic Personality?】\nL206: \nL207: 5\nL208: \nL209: 【68†Google users are less likely to click on links when an AI summary appears in the results】\nL210: \nL211: ## Report Materials\nL212: \nL213:   * 【55†Report PDF】\nL214:   * 【56†Topline】\nL215:   * 【57†Questionnaire】\nL216:   * 【58†American Trends Panel Wave 166】\nL217: ## Table of Contents\nL218: \nL219:   1. 【44†Negative Views of China Have Softened Slightly Among Americans】\nL220:   2. 【49†1. U.S. views of China and Xi】\nL221:   3. 【50†2. Views of China’s role in the world】\nL222:   4. 【51†3. Views of trade between China and the U.S.】\nL223:   5. 【52†4. Views of China as a competitor and threat to the U.S.】\nL224:   6. 【53†Acknowledgments】\nL225:   7. 【54†Methodology】\nL226: \nL227: 901 E St. NW, Suite 300\nL228: Washington, DC 20004\nL229: USA\nL230: (+1) 202-419-4300\nL231: ## Research Topics\nL232: \nL233: 【9†Politics & Policy】\nL234: \nL235: 【10†International Affairs】\nL236: \nL237: 【11†Immigration & Migration】\nL238: \nL239: 【12†Race & Ethnicity】\nL240: \nL241: 【13†Religion】\nL242: \nL243: 【14†Age & Generations】\nL244: \nL245: 【15†Gender & LGBTQ】\nL246: \nL247: 【16†Family & Relationships】\nL248: \nL249: 【17†Economy & Work】\nL250: \nL251: 【18†Science】\nL252: \nL253: 【19†Internet & Technology】\nL254: \nL255: 【20†News Habits & Media】\nL256: \nL257: 【21†Methodological Research】\nL258: ## Follow Us\nL259: \nL260: 【69†ABOUT PEW RESEARCH CENTER】 Pew Research Center is a nonpartisan, nonadvocacy fact tank that informs the public about the issues, attitudes and trends shaping the world. It does not take policy positions. The Center conducts public opinion polling, demographic research, computational social science research and other data-driven research. Pew Research Center is a subsidiary of 【70†The Pew Charitable Trusts†www.pew.org】, its primary funder.\nL261: \nL262: © 2026 Pew Research Center\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow Americans View AI and Its Impact on Human Abilities, Society | Pew Research Center (https://www.pewresearch.org/science/2025/09/17/how-americans-view-ai-and-its-impact-on-people-and-society/)\nciteturn53view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.pewresearch.org/science/2025/09/17/how-americans-view-ai-and-its-impact-on-people-and-society/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 308\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: Numbers, Facts and Trends Shaping Your World\nL3: \nL4:   * 【1†Newsletters】\nL5:   * 【2†Press】\nL6:   * 【3†My Account】\nL7:   * 【4†Donate†pewresearchcenter.networkforgood.com】\nL8:   * 【5†Contacted By Us?】\nL9: \nL10: Read our research on:\nL11: \nL12:   * 【6†Artificial Intelligence】\nL13:   * 【7†Donald Trump】\nL14:   * 【8†Middle East】\nL15: \nL16: [Input: Search pewresearch.org...]\nL17: \nL18:   * Research Topics\nL19: ##### Topics\nL20: \nL21: 【9†Politics & Policy】\nL22: \nL23: 【10†International Affairs】\nL24: \nL25: 【11†Immigration & Migration】\nL26: \nL27: 【12†Race & Ethnicity】\nL28: \nL29: 【13†Religion】\nL30: \nL31: 【14†Age & Generations】\nL32: \nL33: 【15†Gender & LGBTQ】\nL34: \nL35: 【16†Family & Relationships】\nL36: \nL37: 【17†Economy & Work】\nL38: \nL39: 【18†Science】\nL40: \nL41: 【19†Internet & Technology】\nL42: \nL43: 【20†News Habits & Media】\nL44: \nL45: 【21†Methodological Research】\nL46: \nL47: 【22†Full Topic List】\nL48: ##### Regions & Countries\nL49: \nL50: 【23†Asia & the Pacific】\nL51: \nL52: 【24†Europe & Russia】\nL53: \nL54: 【25†Latin America】\nL55: \nL56: 【8†Middle East & North Africa】\nL57: \nL58: 【26†North America】\nL59: \nL60: 【27†Sub-Saharan Africa】\nL61: \nL62: 【28†Multiple Regions / Worldwide】\nL63: ##### Formats\nL64: \nL65: 【29†Feature】\nL66: \nL67: 【30†Fact Sheet】\nL68: \nL69: 【31†Video】\nL70: \nL71: 【32†Data Essay】\nL72: \nL73:   * 【33†Publications】\nL74:   * 【34†Our Methods】\nL75:   * 【35†Short Reads】\nL76:   * 【36†Tools & Datasets】\nL77:   * 【37†Experts】\nL78:   * 【38†About Us】\nL79: \nL80:   *     * 【39†Research Topics】\nL81:     * 【33†Publications】\nL82:     * 【35†Short Reads】\nL83:     * 【40†Tools & Datasets】\nL84:     * 【38†About Pew Research Center】\nL85:     * 【41†Newsletters】\nL86:     * 【42†Press】\nL87:     * 【43†My Account】\nL88:     * 【5†Contacted By Us?】\nL89: \nL90:   * [Input: ]\nL91: \nL92: Read Our Research On:\nL93:     * 【6†Artificial Intelligence】\nL94:     * 【7†Donald Trump】\nL95:     * 【8†Middle East】\nL96: \nL97:   * Report\nL98: \nL99: |\nL100: \nL101: September 17, 2025\nL102: # How Americans View AI and Its Impact on People and Society\nL103: ## Americans see a role for AI in some areas of society but want more control over its use. About half say it’ll erode creative thinking\nL104: \nL105: 【44†Add Us On Google†www.google.com】\nL106: \nL107: By 【45†Brian Kennedy】, 【46†Eileen Yam】, 【47†Emma Kikuchi】, 【48†Isabelle Pula】 and 【49†Javier Fuentes】\nL108: \nL109: Table of Contents\nL110:   1. 【50†How Americans View AI and Its Impact on People and Society】\nL111:   2. 【51†1. AI in Americans’ lives: Awareness, experiences and attitudes】\nL112:   3. 【52†2. Views of AI’s impact on society and human abilities】\nL113:   4. 【53†3. Americans on the risks, benefits of AI – in their own words】\nL114:   5. 【54†Acknowledgments】\nL115:   6. 【55†Methodology】\nL116:   7. 【56†Appendix】\nL117: \nL118: 【57†Image】(SmileStudioAP) How we did this\nL119: Pew Research Center conducted this study to understand Americans’ views of artificial intelligence (AI) and its potential impact on people and society. For this analysis, we surveyed 5,023 adults from June 9 to 15, 2025. Everyone who took part in this survey is a member of the Center’s American Trends Panel (ATP), a group of people recruited through national, random sampling of residential addresses who have agreed to take surveys regularly. This kind of recruitment gives nearly all U.S.\nL120: adults a chance of selection.\nL121: Interviews were conducted either online or by telephone with a live interviewer. The survey is weighted to be representative of the U.S. adult population by gender, race, ethnicity, partisan affiliation, education, presidential vote (among voters) and other factors. 【58†Read more about the ATP’s methodology】.\nL122: \nL123: Here are the 【59†questions used for this report】, the 【60†topline】 and the 【55†survey methodology】.\nL124: #### Key takeaways\nL125:   * Americans are much more concerned than excited about the increased use of AI in daily life, with a majority saying they want more control over how AI is used in their lives.\nL126:   * Far larger shares say AI will erode than improve people’s ability to think creatively and form meaningful relationships.\nL127:   * At the same time, a majority is open to letting AI assist them with day-to-day tasks and activities.\nL128:   * Most Americans don’t support AI playing a role in personal matters such as religion or matchmaking. They’re more open to AI for heavy data analysis, such as for weather forecasting and developing new medicines.\nL129:   * Americans feel strongly that it’s important to be able to tell if pictures, videos or text were made by AI or by humans. Yet many don’t trust their own ability to spot AI-generated content.\nL130: Artificial intelligence tools are now playing a role in many aspects of life and society, spanning politics, the arts, work and beyond. While Americans express some openness to AI’s potential benefits, they’re concerned about its impact on some human abilities, according to a new Pew Research Center survey.\nL131: U.S. adults are generally pessimistic about AI’s effect on people’s ability to think creatively and form meaningful relationships: 53% say AI will worsen people’s ability to think creatively, compared with 16% who say it will improve this. An identical share (16%) says AI will make this skill neither better nor worse.\nL132: \nL133: Far more say AI will worsen rather than improve people’s ability to form meaningful relationships (50% vs. 5%). One-quarter say AI won’t make this better or worse.\nL134: Americans are relatively more optimistic about AI improving problem-solving: 29% of U.S. adults say it will make people better at this skill. Still, a larger share (38%) says AI will make this worse.\nL135: \nL136: Notably, sizable shares of U.S. adults are uncertain about these questions. Between 16% and 20% say they aren’t sure about whether AI will have a positive or negative impact on these human skills.\nL137: Building on our larger body of work tracking public opinion about AI’s societal impact, this study examines how Americans view AI’s effect on human abilities, as well as broader questions about AI’s impact on society. The survey, conducted among 5,023 U.S. adults from June 9 to 15, 2025, also updates our trends on Americans’ AI awareness and attitudes. In addition, in open-ended responses, survey respondents describe in their own words what they see as AI risks and benefits to society.\nL138: ### AI awareness and attitudes\nL139: \nL140: Americans are forming impressions of AI at a time when nearly all U.S. adults (95%) say they have heard at least a little about it.\nL141: \nL142: Overall, Americans continue to be wary about its broader impact:\nL143: \nL144:   * 50% say they’re more concerned than excited about the increased use of AI in daily life, up from 37% in 2021.\nL145:   * 10% are more excited than concerned.\nL146:   * 38% say they’re equally excited and concerned.\nL147: And more than half of Americans (57%) rate the societal risks of AI as high, compared with 25% who say the benefits of AI are high. When asked to describe in their own words why they rated the risks as high, the 【61†most common concern mentioned was about AI weakening human skills and connections】.\nL148: In their own lives, about six-in-ten say they’d like more control over how AI is used, compared with 17% who are comfortable with their amount of control and 21% who are unsure. Still, nearly three-quarters say they’d be willing to let AI assist at least a little with day-to-day tasks and activities.\nL149: ### Detecting content created by AI versus humans\nL150: \nL151: Related to Americans’ desire for more control over AI’s use, most Americans (76%) say it’s extremely or very important to be able to tell if pictures, videos and text were made by AI or people. But 53% of Americans are not too or not at all confident they can detect if something is made by AI versus a person.\nL152: \nL153: Related: 【62†From political speeches to songs, how would Americans react if they found out AI was involved?】\nL154: ### Views on AI playing different roles in society\nL155: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nACT to Move to Single Test Center in South Korea - Newsroom | ACT (https://www.act.org/content/act/en/newsroom/act-to-move-to-single-test-center-in-south-korea.html)\nciteturn53view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.act.org/content/act/en/newsroom/act-to-move-to-single-test-center-in-south-korea.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 30\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: act.org,actprofile.org,act.org,actstudent.org,act.alertline.com,services.actstudent.org,career4.successfactors.com,engage.act.org,discoveractaspire.org,qc.vantage.com,myworkkeys.act.org,twitter.com,facebook.com,youtube.com,plus.google.com,linkedin.com,preview.act.org,workreadycommunities.org,pearson.com,instagram.com,actaspire.org,run2.careerready101.com,run2.keytrain.com,leadershipblog.act.org,my.act.org\nL3: \nL4: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] Search\nL5: \nL6: Search site...\nL7: [Input: Search site...]\nL8: \nL9: Close Search\nL10: \nL11: 【1†Other ACT Services and Products】\nL12: # ACT to Move to Single Test Center in South Korea for December and All Remaining Test Dates in 2016-2017 Testing Year\nL13: \nL14: 【2†IOWA CITY, Iowa】 【2†November 04, 2016】\nL15: IOWA CITY, Iowa— ACT announced today that it will shift all administrations of the ACT® test in South Korea to a single testing site directly monitored by ACT staff for the remainder of the 2016-2017 testing year, starting with the December 10, 2016 test date. All previously existing ACT test centers in the country will be closed for the remainder of the 2016-2017 testing year, which ends in June 2017.\nL16: The move is being made to combat repeated test material breaches in South Korea and strengthen ACT’s ongoing commitment to provide a fair testing environment for all students.\nL17: \nL18: The location of the December test center in South Korea will be the Grand Hilton Hotel in Seoul. Locations for the 2017 test events will be communicated by January 31, 2017.\nL19: Students who have already registered to take the ACT in South Korea in December or on future international test dates will be given the option of testing at the new test center, rescheduling to another test center or test date, or cancelling their registration and obtaining a refund of their registration fees.\nL20: “We can no longer continue to let the criminal actions of a few unscrupulous individuals undermine the efforts of hardworking, honest students” said ACT Chief Commercial Officer Suzana Delanghe. “Moving to a single testing venue directly monitored by ACT staff members will allow ACT to have greater oversight on the testing process, improving test security and fairness.”\nL21: Despite the employment of increased test security measures, ACT has continued to experience deliberate attempts by individuals in South Korea to fraudulently obtain ACT test materials for personal gain. ACT was forced to cancel the June 2016 administration of the ACT in all South Korea and Hong Kong test centers due to a verified breach of the materials.\nL22: ACT’s plan to move to a Computer Adaptive Testing (CAT) design for ACT tests at all international test centers in the future, which was announced earlier this year, remains in place. The CAT design allows for quicker scoring and turnaround of results for examinees and is less vulnerable to certain security threats than the existing paper-and-pencil format.\nL23: #### Welcome to ACT\nL24: \nL25: If you are accessing this site from outside the United States, Puerto Rico, or U.S. Territories, please proceed to the non-U.S. version of our website.\nL26: \nL27: Close   【3†Go†global.act.org】\nL28: \nL29: 【2†Top】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nACT Completes Formation of Public Benefit Corporation and Partnership with Nexus Capital Management (https://industryinsights.act.org/2024/05/act-completes-formation-partnership-nexus)\nciteturn53view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://industryinsights.act.org/2024/05/act-completes-formation-partnership-nexus\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 107\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: [Input]\nL3: \nL4:   * 【1†START YOUR JOURNEY】 START YOUR JOURNEY\nL5: \nL6:     * 【2†Plan for College and Career†www.act.org】\nL7: \nL8:     * 【3†Take the ACT†www.act.org】\nL9: \nL10:     * 【4†School and District Assessment†www.act.org】\nL11: \nL12:     * 【5†Career-Ready Solutions†www.act.org】\nL13: \nL14:   * 【2†Students & Parents†www.act.org】\nL15: \nL16:   * 【6†K-12†www.act.org】\nL17: \nL18:   * 【5†Workforce†www.act.org】\nL19: \nL20:   * 【7†Higher Ed†www.act.org】\nL21: \nL22:   * 【8†Blogs†www.act.org】\nL23: \nL24:   * 【9†MyACT†my.act.org】\nL25: \nL26:   * 【1†START YOUR JOURNEY】 START YOUR JOURNEY\nL27:     * 【2†Plan for College and Career†www.act.org】\nL28: \nL29:     * 【3†Take the ACT†www.act.org】\nL30: \nL31:     * 【4†School and District Assessment†www.act.org】\nL32: \nL33:     * 【5†Career-Ready Solutions†www.act.org】\nL34: \nL35:   * 【2†Students & Parents†www.act.org】\nL36: \nL37:   * 【6†K-12†www.act.org】\nL38: \nL39:   * 【5†Workforce†www.act.org】\nL40: \nL41:   * 【7†Higher Ed†www.act.org】\nL42: \nL43:   * 【8†Blogs†www.act.org】\nL44: \nL45:   * 【9†MyACT†my.act.org】\nL46: \nL47:   * 【1†START YOUR JOURNEY】 START YOUR JOURNEY\nL48: \nL49:     * 【2†Plan for College and Career†www.act.org】\nL50: \nL51:     * 【3†Take the ACT†www.act.org】\nL52:     * 【4†School and District Assessment†www.act.org】\nL53: \nL54:     * 【5†Career-Ready Solutions†www.act.org】\nL55: \nL56:   * 【2†Students & Parents†www.act.org】\nL57: \nL58:   * 【6†K-12†www.act.org】\nL59: \nL60:   * 【5†Workforce†www.act.org】\nL61: \nL62:   * 【7†Higher Ed†www.act.org】\nL63: \nL64:   * 【8†Blogs†www.act.org】\nL65: \nL66:   * 【9†MyACT†my.act.org】\nL67: \nL68: 【10†All posts】\nL69: # ACT Completes Formation of Public Benefit Corporation and Partnership with Nexus Capital Management\nL70: \nL71: May 01, 2024\nL72: \nL73: ACT\nL74: \nL75: Company will transform college and career pathways for students, educators, and employers\nL76: IOWA CITY, Iowa — ACT, Inc., the mission-driven provider of the assessments, research, and work-ready credentials designed to support education and workplace success, today announced that it has completed its formation of a new Delaware public benefit corporation and closed on its partnership with Nexus Capital Management, a Los Angeles-based private equity firm.\nL77: Janet Godwin will continue as CEO of the new company, which will be known as ACT Education Corp. (temporarily known as Impact Asset Corp.). The new public benefit corporation combines ACT and Encoura, an education data science and research organization that was a wholly owned subsidiary of ACT. ACT Education Corp.\nL78: will maintain all of its current offerings, including its flagship ACT college and career readiness assessment, and continue to offer its readiness assessments, enrollment insights, and workforce credential solutions to its thousands of partners globally.\nL79: “Today marks the beginning of a new era for ACT,” said ACT CEO Janet Godwin. “Students, job seekers, and educators around the world will benefit from the new products, solutions, and services that we will create to better meet the needs of people at any stage in their education or career paths. Our goal is to help more students be ready for their postsecondary paths, and help more adults advance in their careers.”\nL80: In addition to Damian Giangiacomo and Evan Glucoft of Nexus Capital, other board members of ACT Education Corp. will include Bethlam Forsa, CEO of Savvas Learning; Steven Tapp, an expert with significant experience in the assessment industry; Jamie Merisotis, president and CEO of Lumina Foundation; and Janet Godwin.\nL81: The continuation of an Iowa nonprofit organization, headquartered in Iowa City, is being partially funded by proceeds from the transaction. Over the next year, the nonprofit will determine a name, its full structure, and provide details on its charitable mission.\nL82: Macquarie Capital acted as exclusive financial advisor to ACT, and Morrison & Foerster LLP served as ACT’s legal advisor. Nyemaster Goode P.C. and Polsinelli PC also acted as legal advisors to ACT. Milbank LLP acted as legal advisor to Nexus Capital Management LP.\nL83: ###\nL84: About ACT\nL85: ACT is transforming college and career readiness pathways so that everyone can discover and fulfill their potential. Grounded in more than 65 years of research, ACT’s learning resources, assessments, research, and work-ready credentials are trusted by students, job seekers, educators, schools, government agencies, and employers in the U.S. and around the world to help people achieve their education and career goals at every stage of life. Visit us at 【11†www.act.org†www.act.org】.\nL86: Contact: ACT Public Relations; publicrelations@act.org\nL87: [ACT Products and Updates, From Our Leaders]\nL88: \nL89:   * 【2†Students & Parents†www.act.org】\nL90:   * 【6†K-12 Professionals†www.act.org】\nL91:   * 【7†Higher Education†www.act.org】\nL92:   * 【5†Workforce†www.act.org】\nL93:   * 【12†International†www.act.org】\nL94: \nL95:   * 【13†About ACT†www.act.org】\nL96:   * 【14†Careers†www.act.org】\nL97:   * 【15†Events†www.act.org】\nL98:   * 【16†Solutions & Services†www.act.org】\nL99:   * 【17†State & Federal Programs†www.act.org】\nL100:   * 【8†ACT Blogs†www.act.org】\nL101: